seg-7f4957a488b7 = Dependency-free <b>SGP4/SDP4</b> (SGP4: Simplified General Perturbations { "4" }; SDP4: Simplified Deep-space Perturbations { "4" }), validated to { "4.12" } mm vs all { "666" } AIAA (American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics) 2006-6753 vectors (position <b>and</b> velocity, the latter to { "1" } mm/s); analytic Keplerian + J2-secular; inertial velocity exposed and surfaced <b>end-to-end</b> via the runnable <b><c1/> scenario</b> (<c2/>, reachable from CLI/Python/WASM/MCP (CLI: command-line interface; WASM: WebAssembly; MCP: Model Context Protocol)) — per step it emits the TEME (true equator, mean equinox) <b>and</b> GCRS (Geocentric Celestial Reference System) state (position + velocity), the ITRF/ECEF (ITRF: International Terrestrial Reference Frame; ECEF: Earth-centred, Earth-fixed) position, the WGS-84 (WGS: World Geodetic System) <b>sub-satellite ground track</b> (lat/lon/alt), and station azimuth/elevation/range + <b>range-rate/Doppler</b> (the radial range-rate matches the true position derivative JD-noise-free (JD: Julian date) to { "6e-9" } m/s) — and via the CCSDS (Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems) <b>OEM</b> (Orbit Ephemeris Message) velocity-carrying ephemeris export (<c3/>). A <b>numerical (Cowell) propagator</b> (<c4/>) integrates a configurable <b>zonal-gravity</b> force model — the <b>full Earth zonal field through degree { "6" }</b> (<c5/>, the published EGM-96 (EGM: Earth Gravitational Model) <c6/>) — with a choice of adaptive driver — RK4 (fourth-order Runge–Kutta) <b>step doubling</b> or the <b>Dormand–Prince RK5({ "4" })</b> embedded pair (<c7/> / <c8/>, a cheaper 7-eval embedded error estimate that clears the same Kepler gate and agrees with the RK4 path to <{ "1" } m on a J2..J6 orbit) — pinned against <b>analytic truth stronger than a numerical cross-tool</b>: the unperturbed orbit reproduces the <b>exact universal-variable Kepler solution to sub-metre over { "24" } h</b> (a tighter gate than the milestone's "vs reference < { "10" } m"), energy + angular momentum conserve to ~{ "1e-9" } relative, the J2 nodal regression matches the closed-form secular rate to first-order theory, the zonal acceleration is verified to be the <b>exact gradient of its own potential</b> (and to reduce to the 666-vector-validated J2 closed form), and <c9/> <b>returns <c10/> rather than a wrong answer</b> when Newton fails to converge (e.g. near-perigee e = { "0.999" }). <b>Epoch-driven third-body (Sun and Moon)</b> gravity is <b>wired into the time-varying integrator</b> (<c11/> / <c12/>, with the <c13/> Montenbruck & Gill low-precision Sun and Moon ephemerides — no DE/SPK (DE: Development Ephemeris; SPK: planetary ephemeris kernel) kernel needed): each RHS (right-hand side) evaluation samples the perturber positions at the advanced epoch <c14/>, so the Sun/Moon move along their orbits during the integration. The perturbation is verified as the exact gradient of its disturbing potential, vanishes at the geocentre, and has the textbook LEO (low Earth orbit) magnitudes (~{ "5e-7" } m/s² Sun, ~{ "1.1e-6" } m/s² Moon); the Sun ephemeris hits hand-derived J2000 anchors (perihelion distance, ~−{ "23" }° solstice declination, ~{ "1" }°/day motion) and the Moon ephemeris its perigee/apogee envelope, ~{ "384 400" } km mean distance, ≤ { "5.3" }° inclination bound, and sidereal-month direction return; and the integration wiring is proven <b>bit-exact</b> (RHS term equals <c15/> at the sampled position at t = { "0" } and t = { "1" } day) with a quarter-year epoch shift yielding a different trajectory. <b>Solar-radiation pressure</b> (SRP) rides the same epoch-driven RHS (<c16/> / <c17/>): the cannonball model <c18/> with a <c19/> umbra+penumbra eclipse factor (smooth ν ∈ [{ "0,1" }], verified to taper through a monotonic penumbra that extends beyond the umbral cylinder), verified against the textbook 1-AU (AU: astronomical unit) pressure (≈ { "4.54e-6" } N/m²), the ~{ "1.36e-7" } m/s² LEO magnitude pushing away from the Sun, the inverse-square fall-off (doubling Sun distance quarters it), an <b>exactly-zero</b> acceleration in the umbral cylinder, and a propagated displacement that scales ~linearly with A/m. <b>Atmospheric drag</b> is the first velocity-dependent term (<c20/> / <c21/> + the new <c22/> / velocity-passing RHS): quadratic drag <c23/> against the co-rotating atmosphere <c24/>, with <c25/> the Vallado piecewise-exponential <c26/> — verified by the { "1.225" } kg/m³ sea-level anchor, a strictly monotonic LEO decay with a physical ~{ "58" } km scale height, the ~{ "2e-6" } m/s² drag magnitude opposing the relative velocity, and the <b>dissipation signature</b> (a { "300" } km orbit loses specific energy monotonically and its semi-major axis decays a bounded ~km/day, where the vacuum orbit conserves energy). The <b>post-Newtonian (Schwarzschild) relativistic correction</b> is the second velocity-dependent term (<c27/> / <c28/>): the IERS (International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service) <c29/> form <c30/>, the leading driver of the relativistic perigee advance — verified against its closed-form circular value <c31/> (radial, outward), its textbook <c32/> LEO ratio to two-body, the hand-simplified radial-velocity form, and the <b>conservative signature</b> (it perturbs the orbit but holds the semi-major axis to under a metre/day, the structural opposite of drag). The <b>Lense–Thirring</b> frame-dragging term (<c33/> / <c34/>, IERS { "2010" } §{ "10" }) is the gravitomagnetic correction beyond Schwarzschild — opt-in, ~{ "1" }–{ "2" } orders of magnitude below it at LEO, verified to vanish without Earth rotation and to sit below the Schwarzschild magnitude. <b>Orbit determination</b> (OD, <c35/>) closes the loop from observations back to a state: a <b>Gauss–Newton batch</b> corrector (<c36/>) drives a candidate <c37/> onto the best fit of ground-station ranges propagated across the arc (sub-metre / mm·s⁻¹ from noiseless ranges; ~{ "2" } m with a post-fit residual at the { "5" } m noise floor — the signature of a consistent least-squares fit), with a <b>sequential</b> recursive variant on the unscented filter

