seg-0cf86b61224c = A <b>stochastic time-spoof detector</b> (<c1/> kind, <c2/> + <c3/>): four injection shapes (linear ramp, step jump, meaconing, replay) against a clock-aided monitor with a two-sided <b>χ²₁ energy / Neyman–Pearson test</b>, the detection threshold set from a target false-alarm budget <c4/>, and the <b>missed-detection probability <c5/> reported both closed-form and by Monte-Carlo</b> (the two agree to a few ×1/√N) — the Security figure of merit is <c6/>. Plus a <b>link-budget jamming model</b> (<c7/> kind, <c8/>): jammer-to-signal ratio from geometry (free-space path loss + per-direction receive-antenna gain), the standard <b>anti-jam effective-C/N₀ equation</b> (despreading processing gain + spectral-separation factor <c9/>; Kaplan & Hegarty §{ "9.4" }), per-satellite loss-of-lock at a configurable tracking threshold, and an <c10/> figure of merit over a Walker constellation. Plus a <b>multi-layer RF/measurement (RF: radio-frequency) spoof-monitor suite</b> (<c11/>): a <b>multi-satellite RAIM-consistency</b> parity test (<c12/>, weighted residual SOS (sum of squares) vs a χ²(m−{ "4" }) threshold — and it correctly flags that a common-mode bias is RAIM-invisible), an <b>AGC (automatic gain control) monitor</b> (<c13/>, received-power excess over the expected floor), a <b>signal-quality / SQM (signal-quality monitoring) monitor</b> (<c14/>, Early/Late correlator imbalance), and a layer-fusion combiner (<c15/>). These compose into an <b>integrated <c16/></b> whose <c17/> ingests one epoch of observables (geometry + residuals, AGC power, Early/Late taps) and runs all three layers through the weighted fusion in a single call, returning the per-layer diagnostics; it is exposed as the runnable <b><c18/> scenario</b> (<c19/>, CLI / Python / playground reachable, JSON + SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics)) and <b>characterised against the published TEXBAT (Texas Spoofing Test Battery) scenario parameters</b> (Humphreys et al., ION (Institute of Navigation) GNSS