seg-431b3f05c08c = <c1/>: a cold-atom <b>gravimeter measurement model</b> whose white-noise floor is derived from the CAI accelerometer ASD (<c2/>), a low-degree <b>fully-normalised spherical-harmonic gravity-anomaly field</b> (validated against the closed-form Legendre functions and a hand-derived single-term anomaly) plus synthetic <b>mascons</b>, and a <b>gravity-map-matching particle filter</b> (composing <c3/> + <c4/>) that recovers a GPS-denied track from the anomaly sequence it flies through. The <b>60-minute GPS-denied benchmark</b> (<c5/>, <c6/>) flies a ~{ "700" } km / one-hour outage where the inertial solution drifts to <b>≈ { "70" } km</b>, and a <b>hierarchical coarse-to-fine</b> matcher with the gravimeter's deterministic seeded white noise recovers it to <b>≈ { "145" } m (< { "500" } m)</b> — stable across noise realisations, and provably refinement-limited (a single coarse grid stalls at ~{ "2" } km). Plus a <b>full IGRF-14 (IGRF: International Geomagnetic Reference Field) geomagnetic main-field model</b> (<c7/>: the IAGA Schmidt-normalised spherical-harmonic field to degree/order { "13" }, { "2025.0" } + secular variation, coefficients machine-generated from the official <c8/>; validated against the closed-form tilted dipole, a finite-difference of the scalar potential, and the known geomagnetic pole/strength) — the magnetic field a platform can map-match for magnetic-anomaly navigation. Plus <b>terrain-referenced navigation (TERCOM/SITAN (TERCOM: terrain contour matching; SITAN: Sandia Inertial Terrain-Aided Navigation))</b> and the <b>combined gravity+magnetic+terrain navigator</b> (<c9/>): a hand-rolled SRTM (Shuttle Radar Topography Mission) <c10/> digital-elevation-model (DEM) parser (16-bit signed big-endian, row-major, north-row-first, void -32768; validated against the GDAL (Geospatial Data Abstraction Library) SRTMHGT (Shuttle Radar Topography Mission height-file format) driver spec and a closed-form bilinear-midpoint oracle), a radar/baro <b>altimeter</b> model and <c11/> matcher that recovers a GPS-denied track from the ground-elevation profile against the DEM, and <c12/> which fuses <b>three scalar field channels</b> per waypoint (Δg mGal · |B| nT crustal-anomaly · elevation m) so the joint posterior is sharper (lower CRLB (Cramér–Rao lower bound)) than any single field — bounded < { "500" } m over the 60-min outage and, in expectation, no worse than the best single channel. Plus <b>sequential (recursive) terrain-referenced navigation — SITAN as a running filter</b> (<c13/>, <c14/>): the same altimeter-vs-DEM measurement model run <b>epoch by epoch</b> through the <c15/> SIR (sampling importance resampling) engine (predict by the INS increment + process noise → reweight by the terrain match → resample on degeneracy), so a <b>time-varying</b> INS drift is <em>tracked</em> along the track where the batch matcher only recovers a single constant offset — the recursive estimate stays bounded and re-converges (final ≈ { "70" } m) while the unaided solution diverges unbounded to ≈ { "5" } km, with per-epoch error following terrain distinctiveness and an effective-sample-size health monitor. Honest scope: the map is <b>known and fixed</b> — recursive <em>localization</em> against a stored DEM (the localization half of terrain SLAM (simultaneous localisation and mapping)); joint map estimation is the separate RBPF below. All four are now <b>scenario-engine <c16/> wired</b> (<c17/>, <c18/>, <c19/>, <c20/>) with an SVG drift chart (<c21/>, <c22/>, <c23/>). <b>{ "0.36" } (WP-10):</b> the <c24/> navigator takes an opt-in real reference field (<c25/>: bundled EGM2008 to degree { "70" }, or an ICGEM <c26/> file), whose anomaly synthesis matches the ICGEM Calculation Service to about { "1e-8" } mGal (VALIDATED, <c27/>); on that degree-70 field the 60-min benchmark ends at { "3106" } m, so the < { "500" } m bar is NOT MET (pre-registered and predicted: degree { "70" } is too smooth). An opt-in map-error Monte Carl